azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net.  Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.

And awaaaay we go!

> [1]: putting on a ring of levitation while standing on a sink should
> not let you levitate in the first place, thus doing no damage.
> --dtriscuit

Not bad.  Of course, stepping off the space with the ring on will throw
the player into the air....

> [2]: Make people in pits near-immune to projectiles (~80% chance to
> miss), so that rangers and wizards can dig down to form bunkers. Hell
> yeah, son. --Azeral

This one's already been covered in #15.  This is one of the best ideas
I've seen since these compilations started, providing a good defense,
with suitable drawbacks, against a variety of attacks including random
wands carried by monsters, winter wolves, hellhounds, and dragons.

> [3]: cursed+confused ?oLight makes the whole current level dark
> --Xarann

Hm, interesting.  Potentially useful too.  Could be a big problem on
Sokoban, but the player probably shouldn't be IDing stuff there anyway.
 I like it.

> [4]: The color for the floating eyes needs to be lighter --Gaulgath

As others have said, this is a monitor problem.

> [5]: rubbing a stone against a /oPoly should polymorph the stone
> --Botje

Interesting.  If this is put in though, a charge from the wand should
be expended, and of course conduct should be lost as well.

> [6]: Lizard scale mail - Lizards have a 25% chance of dropping scales
> when killed. Lizard scales are stackable and wearable; if you are
> wearing 5 or more, it may be enchanted up to mail status. Lizard scale
> mail has the same protection as an orcish chain mail, and provides
> stoning resistance. --TheShrewdDude_

Kinda interesting.  How much does it weigh though?  The fact that it's
fairly poor armor is a suitable balance.

> [7]: Even before the top of Rodney's tower, there ought to be another
> fake tower without a moat. This tower has a non-secret door, as well as
> a vampire lord and hell hound inside. Between them sits Fake Rodney: a
> doppelganger wearing an amulet of unchanging. --TheShrewdDude

The fake towers are a reference to prior versions of Nethack, in which
the Wizard was waiting in the middle of the space.  This is potentially
a further reference to those days, which, of course, I like
tremendously.

> [8]: Helmet description: opera mask.  Wear with a cloak of description
> "opera cloak" for a bonus or penalty (and YAFMs when hallucinating).
> --Grunt

What is the bonus or penalty?  What is the YAFM?

> [9]: Having many uranium wands in your inventory abuses your
> constitution. Having a lot of them kills you. "Your uranium wands get
> hot.". "Joe, killed by elementary nuclear physics". --Avian

Interesting.  I generally like ideas that take advantage of the
descriptions of items, and I generally like ideas that provide
unlooked-for sources of peril.  If wands of digging are uranium this
could be very dangerous.  What kind of radiation shielding do bags of
holding provide?

Even so, I suspect that just having multiple wands isn't enough to
cause a reaction.

> [10]: But if you happen to be wearing an "oLS, it's a crude but
> effective form of crowd control. --cjeris

Huh?

> [11]: if female, t-shirts should give a boost to charisma when recently
> gotten wet (pool of water, rust trap, etc) --Smeghead_

Charisma is not just physical attractiveness.  I'd say no.

> [12]: Zombies should be greedy for mummy wrappings. If there is one
> nearby, it should try to wear it and turn into the appropriate type of
> mummy. Mummies should have greater bonuses over zombies for this
> reason. --TheShrewdDude

I kind of like this idea.  Maybe I've just been playing too much Shiren
the Wanderer though, which has frequent monster upgrades, but I like
that element of sudden escalation of danger.

> [13]: squeaky doors that act like squeaky board traps when you open
> them --iank

Good idea.

> [14]: The 'G' command should follow paths in the gehennom mazes along
> their twists and turns until you encounter an actual fork, monster or
> object. --Smello

Note that there is a trick to exploring the mazes of Gehennom that, if
the feature does make use of it, is a minor spoiler, and if it doesn't
use it, then no knowledgeable player will use it.  (The trick is to
travel through zig-zag passages *diagonally*, cutting the number of
moves wasted by half.)

> [15]: pushing boulders into water with 'submerged' (hidden, unknown,
> whatever) water creatures should hurt/kill them. --iank

I really like this idea, since it brings parity with both drawbridge
behavior and the general dangerousness of pools.  Of course this should
affect the player too, making giants (like the titan on one version of
Medusa) exceptionally dangerous around pools and moats.

This, however, would bring up the question, why don't thrown boulders
instantly kill the player?  I'd say that the boulder-in-pool situation
doesn't kill by crushing so much as by *drowning*.

> [16]: Tins can contain spam. maybe YAFM when hallu? --Botje

Problem here is that Spam is a trademarked name.

> [17]: Form of item erosion: denting.  "You see here a thoroughly dented
> pot." --Grunt

What are the game effects if an item is dented?  I like the idea that
dented wands shoot crooked....

> [18]: Amulet of the prism. Grants reflection with the special trait
> that you get one reflection for each distinct source of reflection you
> have. --cjeris

What does it mean to have "more than one reflection"?  Reflection
already reflects anything that comes into your square.

> [19]: A Unicode version. A-Za-z just isn't enough. --nornagon
Nethack is played on a very wide variety of systems, so I expect this
would be harder to implement than you think.

- John H.



